al risk and didn't evacuate from the water. - **Hurricane Sandy (2012):** Not a rare outlier — it was a massive system that killed over 200 people in the U.S. and caused $70 billion in damage. At landfall in New Jersey, it was technically post-tropical with Category 1-equivalent winds. If people had only heard "post-tropical" or "Category 1," they might have stayed. The surge was catastrophic and drove the entire disaster. - **Hurricane Ike (2008):** Category 2 at landfall in Texas. Killed over 100 people directly and indirectly. The storm surge was 15–20 feet, on par with a Category 4-5. If people had assumed "only a Cat 2, I'll be fine," they drowned. - **Hurricane Florence (2018):** Made landfall in North Carolina as a Category 1. The wind damage was minimal, but it stalled and dumped 30+ inches of rain, causing catastrophic inland flooding that killed dozens and cost billions. The category number communicated "not a major hurricane," but the water disaster was extreme. - **Hurricane Harvey (2017):** Landfall in Texas as a Category 4 — so wind damage was catastrophic right there. But the truly unprecedented, $125 billion disaster was the 60 inches of rain and massive flooding that occurred after it was downgraded to a tropical storm. Most of the damage and suffering happened when the storm was no longer a hurricane at all. The Category 4 at landfall only tells a fraction of the story. 3. **Now, why does this matter for Dorian?** You asked why Dorian was considered the worst hurricane to hit Florida in decades even though Irma arrived as a Category 5. As we established, Dorian didn't actually hit. But the fear that generated that label was precisely because the forecast wasn't just "Category 5 winds." It was "a Category 5 that is going to stall and push a historic storm surge onto the coast while dropping absurd amounts of rain." The wind number told people to evacuate from the wind, which they should have. But the reason meteorologists and emergency managers were